nerabilities found have been LOW or MEDIUM severity, with the most recent HIGH severity CVE in October 2023.\",\n   115→    \"author\": \"Daniel Stenberg (via Simon Willison)\",\n   116→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   117→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/26/pressure/\",\n   118→    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-26\",\n   119→    \"topics\": [\"security\", \"ai\", \"generative-ai\", \"llms\", \"curl\", \"ai-security-research\"],\n   120→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   121→  },\n   122→  {\n   123→    \"title\": \"Microsoft Copilot Cowork Exfiltrates Files\",\n   124→    \"description\": \"Microsoft Copilot Cowork allowed agents to send emails to the user's inbox without approval, but those messages could leak data to attackers via rendered images. Because messages can contain external images that trigger network requests, data can be exfiltrated when a user opens a compromised message. OneDrive can create pre-authenticated download links that could be leaked via prompt injection.\",\n   125→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   126→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   127→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/26/copilot-cowork-exfiltration/\",\n   128→    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-26\",\n   129→    \"topics\": [\"microsoft\", \"security\", \"ai\", \"prompt-injection\", \"generative-ai\", \"llms\", \"exfiltration-attacks\"],\n   130→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   131→  },\n   132→  {\n   133→    \"title\": \"Paul Graham on AI-written emails from founders\",\n   134→    \"description\": \"Paul Graham criticizes the trend of founders using AI to write emails in a hard-hitting journalistic style, noting he has never finished reading an email signed by a human but written by AI. He describes it as feeling like being lied to, making him think less of the author who can't write well unaided and are trying to trick him.\",\n   135→    \"author\": \"Paul Graham (via Simon Willison)\",\n   136→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   137→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/26/paul-graham-ai-writing/\",\n   138→    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-26\",\n   139→    \"topics\": [\"writing\", \"ai-misuse\", \"generative-ai\", \"ai\", \"llms\"],\n   140→    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n   141→  },\n   142→  {\n   143→    \"title\": \"Notes on Pope Leo XIV's encyclical on AI\",\n   144→    \"description\": \"The Vatican released 'Magnifica Humanitas' by Pope Leo XIV on safeguarding the human person in the time of artificial intelligence. Simon describes it as some of the clearest writing he's seen on the ethics of integrating AI into modern society.\",\n   145→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   146→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   147→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/25/pope-ai-encyclical/\",\n   148→    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-25\",\n   149→    \"topics\": [\"predictions\", \"ai\", \"kakapo\", \"generative-ai\", \"llms\", \"ai-ethics\"],\n   150→    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n   151→  },\n   152→  {\n   153→    \"title\": \"Armin Ronacher on AI-generated GitHub issues\",\n   154→    \"description\": \"Armin Ronacher expresses frustration with people submitting issues not in their own voice - problems thrown into an LLM that reworded them into messes of inaccurate conclusions, fake-minimal repros, and wrong implementation strategies. He prefers issue reports condensed to what the human actually observed: the command run, expected behavior, what happened instead, and exact error/logs.\",\n   155→    \"author\": \"Armin Ronacher (via Simon Willison)\",\n   156→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   157→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/24/armin-ronacher-github-issues/\",\n   158→    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-24\",\n   159→    \"topics\": [\"ai\", \"github-issues\", \"llms\", \"ai-ethics\", \"open-source\", \"coding-agents\", \"generative-ai\"],\n   160→    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n   161→  },\n   162→  {\n   163→    \"title\": \"FTC Settles Charges Over Deceptive 'Active Listening' AI Marketing\",\n   164→    \"description\": \"FTC requires Cox Media Group and two other firms to pay nearly $1 million to settle charges they deceived customers about an 'Active Listening' AI-powered marketing service. The companies claimed to listen to consumer conversations through smart devices, but actually just resold email lists from other data brokers at a markup. FTC clarified that clicking through terms of service doesn't constitute adequate consent for invasive data collection.\",\n   165→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   166→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   167→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/22/ftc-active-listening/\",\n   168→    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-22\",\n   169→    \"topics\": [\"privacy\", \"microphone-ads-conspiracy\", \"ftc\", \"ai-ethics\"],\n   170→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   171→  },\n   172→  {\n   173→    \"title\": \"The memory shortage is causing a repricing of consumer electronics\",\n   174→    \"description\": \"David Oks explains why consumer products using memory will get more expensive. Memory manufacturers (only three large companies remain) have fixed wafer capacity split between DDR, LPDDR, and HBM. HBM for GPUs grew from 2% to expected 20% of wafer allocation by end of 2026, and HBM consumes more than 3x the wafer capacity per GB than DDR or LPDDR. This already impacts sub-$100 smartphones important to African and South Asian markets.\",\n   175→    \"author\": \"David Oks (via Simon Willison)\",\n   176→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   177→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/22/memory-shortage/\",\n   178→    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-22\",\n   179→    \"topics\": [\"memory\", \"ai\", \"ai-ethics\", \"hardware\"],\n   180→    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n   181→  },\n   182→  {\n   183→    \"title\": \"Datasette Agent - extensible AI assistant for Datasette\",\n   184→    \"description\": \"Simon announced the first release of Datasette Agent, a new extensible AI assistant for Datasette. After working on his LLM Python library for just over three years, this represents the moment LLM and Datasette finally come together.\",\n   185→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   186→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   187→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/21/datasette-agent/\",\n   188→    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-21\",\n   189→    \"topics\": [\"sqlite\", \"ai\", \"datasette\", \"generative-ai\", \"llms\", \"llm\", \"uv\", \"datasette-agent\"],\n   190→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   191→  },\n   192→  {\n   193→    \"title\": \"SpaceX S-1: Anthropic paying $1.25B/month for compute capacity\",\n   194→    \"description\": \"SpaceX filing reveals Cloud Services Agreements with Anthropic for access to compute capacity across COLOSSUS and COLOSSUS II. Anthropic agreed to pay $1.25 billion per month through May 2029, with capacity ramping in May-June 2026 at reduced fee. Agreements terminable by either party upon 90 days notice.\",\n   195→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   196→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   197→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/20/spacex-anthropic-deal/\",\n   198→    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-20\",\n   199→    \"topics\": [\"anthropic\", \"grok\", \"generative-ai\", \"ai\", \"llms\", \"infrastructure\"],\n   200→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   201→  },\n   202→  {\n   203→    \"title\": \"Gemini 3.5 Flash: more expensive, but Google plan to use it for everything\",\n   204→    \"description\": \"Google released Gemini 3.5 Flash at Google I/O, skipping the preview modifier and going straight to general availability. Google appears to be using it for many key products. Simon discusses pricing and implications.\",\n   205→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   206→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   207→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/19/gemini-3.5-flash/\",\n   208→    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-19\",\n   209→    \"topics\": [\"google\", \"ai\", \"generative-ai\", \"llms\", \"gemini\", \"llm-pricing\"],\n   210→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   211→  },\n   212→  {\n   213→    \"title\": \"The last six months in LLMs in five minutes\",\n   214→    \"description\": \"Simon's annotated slides from his five minute lightning talk at PyCon US 2026, covering major developments in LLMs over the previous six months.\",\n   215→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   216→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   217→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/19/six-months-llms-five-minutes/\",\n   218→    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-19\",\n   219→    \"topics\": [\"lightning-talks\", \"pycon\", \"speaking\", \"ai\", \"generative-ai\", \"local-llms\", \"llms\", \"coding-agents\"],\n   220→    \"type\": \"tutorial\"\n   221→  },\n   222→  {\n   223→    \"title\": \"GDS weighs in on the NHS's decision to retreat from Open Source\",\n   224→    \"description\": \"UK Government Digital Service published 'AI, open code and vulnerability risk in the public sector' recommending to keep open by default. Making everything private adds delivery and policy costs, and can reduce reuse and scrutiny. Terence Eden interprets this as a major escalation in the disagreement over NHS closing repos in response to Project Glasswing vulnerabilities.\",\n   225→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   226→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   227→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/17/gds-nhs-open-source/\",\n   228→    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-17\",\n   229→    \"topics\": [\"open-source\", \"security\", \"ai\", \"generative-ai\", \"llms\", \"gov-uk\", \"ai-ethics\", \"ai-security-research\"],\n   230→    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n   231→  }\n   232→]\n   233→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"
      }
    ]
